          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is there a link between sleep and migraine?",
          "answer": "Yes, according to the retrieved material, sleep and migraine are linked in both directions. Poor sleep can make attacks more likely, attacks can disturb sleep, and sleep is one of the more reliable ways an attack ends. (Source: \"Sleep and migraine: regularity, and the relationship running both ways,\" general clinical reference summary.)\n\nThe material suggests that changes in sleep timing matter more than the total amount. Both too little sleep and sleeping in can trigger attacks. The example given is a late Friday night followed by a long Saturday lie-in, which can bring on weekend headaches. The same source notes that sleep pattern is one of the more commonly reported triggers and one that is fairly easy to change.\n\nOther retrieved material covers the wider context. The general migraine summary lists addressing sleep, meal regularity and overuse of acute medication as part of managing migraine. It also lists warning signs that need urgent medical assessment, such as a sudden severe headache, a headache with fever and a stiff neck, headache after a head injury, new weakness or confusion, or a new headache type after age 50. If any of these apply to you, seek urgent care now.\n\nThe results did not cover how sleep problems are treated in people who have migraine, or whether a specific sleep disorder such as sleep apnea is connected to migraine. If you are concerned about your own sleep or headaches, a doctor or pharmacist can discuss them with you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
